    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity>
    <title>Triangular Bridge Design Project</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Work in pairs or small groups to design a model bridge using triangular supports and apply trigonometric concepts to ensure structural stability and accuracy.
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Bridge Design Challenge.</alert> Your task is to design a bridge that spans a gap of <m>60\text{ cm}</m> and can support a load. The bridge must use triangular support structures (trusses) because triangles are the most stable geometric shapes.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Choose one of the following bridge designs:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><strong>Design A (Warren Truss):</strong> A series of equilateral triangles placed side by side.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>Design B (Pratt Truss):</strong> Triangles with varying angles and side lengths for added strength.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>Design C (Custom Design):</strong> Create your own triangular pattern, justifying your choice with trigonometric calculations.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Provide images or diagrams of different truss designs. Emphasize that real bridge engineers use trigonometry to calculate angles and lengths to ensure the bridge can distribute weight evenly and withstand forces. Discuss why triangles cannot change shape under load (unlike rectangles), making them ideal for construction.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Trigonometric Calculations for Your Bridge.</alert> Complete the calculations table below. Using trigonometric ratios and the cosine rule, calculate the side lengths and angles needed for your bridge design.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Example scenario:</strong> If you design a bridge with triangular sections where each triangle has:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>A base of <m>10\text{ cm}</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>Two equal sides (isosceles triangle)</p></li>
                        <li><p>An apex angle of <m>60°</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                    Calculate the lengths of the two equal sides using the cosine rule.
                </p>
                <table xml:id="bridge-calculations-table">
                    <title>Bridge Truss Calculations</title>
                    <tabular halign="left">
                        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                            <cell>Triangle Section</cell>
                            <cell>Given Information</cell>
                            <cell>Trigonometric Method</cell>
                            <cell>Calculated Side Length(s)</cell>
                            <cell>Calculated Angle(s)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Truss 1</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Truss 2</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Truss 3</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell>Truss 4</cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                            <cell></cell>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
                <solution component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        For an isosceles triangle with base <m>a = 10\text{ cm}</m>, two equal sides <m>b = c</m>, and apex angle <m>C = 60°</m>:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Using the cosine rule rearranged for the equal sides:
                    </p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\cos A</mrow>
                        <mrow>\text{Since } b = c \text{ and the apex angle is } 60°:</mrow>
                        <mrow>10^2 = b^2 + b^2 - 2b^2\cos 60°</mrow>
                        <mrow>100 = 2b^2 - 2b^2(0.5)</mrow>
                        <mrow>100 = 2b^2 - b^2 = b^2</mrow>
                        <mrow>b = c = 10\text{ cm}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    <p>
                        Students should follow similar procedures for each truss section in their design, recording all calculations clearly.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Scale Drawing of Your Bridge Design.</alert> Create a detailed scale drawing of your bridge showing:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>All triangular trusses with accurate dimensions (use your calculated values)</p></li>
                        <li><p>All angles labeled in degrees</p></li>
                        <li><p>A clear scale (e.g., 1 cm on paper = 5 cm actual size)</p></li>
                        <li><p>Labels for all sides and angles</p></li>
                        <li><p>Deck (the roadway) supported by the trusses</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Learners can use:
                        <ul>
                            <li>Graph paper and ruler for traditional drawings</li>
                            <li>GeoGebra or Desmos for dynamic geometric constructions</li>
                            <li>CAD software (free versions like LibreCAD) for professional designs</li>
                        </ul>
                        The drawing should demonstrate understanding of scale and proportional relationships.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Build a Model Bridge.</alert> Using your scale drawing, construct a physical model of your bridge using:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Straws, wooden sticks, or dowels</p></li>
                        <li><p>String, fishing line, or adhesive tape for joining</p></li>
                        <li><p>A cardboard or foam board deck</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Ensure that:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>All angles match your calculated values (within reason)</p></li>
                        <li><p>All sides have lengths proportional to your calculations</p></li>
                        <li><p>Joints are secure and stable</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This phase connects abstract mathematics to concrete construction. Learners will experience how small errors in angle or length can affect overall stability. Discuss how real engineers use precision to ensure safety.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Load Testing and Analysis.</alert> Test your bridge model by carefully placing weights on the deck (e.g., books, sand bags, or water bottles) until it fails or reaches a maximum load. Record:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Maximum load supported (in kg or number of objects)</p></li>
                        <li><p>Which part of the bridge failed first (if any)</p></li>
                        <li><p>Whether the failure matched your predictions based on trigonometric stress analysis</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Connect this to real engineering concepts: 
                        <ul>
                            <li>Force distribution in triangular structures</li>
                            <li>Compression and tension in different parts of the truss</li>
                            <li>Why certain angles are stronger than others</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Analysis and Reflection Questions.</alert> Answer the following questions based on your bridge design and testing:
                </p>
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li><p>How did you use the sine rule or cosine rule to determine the dimensions of your bridge trusses? Show at least one complete calculation.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Why are triangles better for bridge construction than other shapes like rectangles or circles? Use geometric and physical reasoning.</p></li>
                    <li><p>If your bridge failed during load testing, what trigonometric adjustments could you make to improve its strength? (e.g., changing angles, increasing side lengths)</p></li>
                    <li><p>Research and describe how real bridges (such as the Sydney Harbour Bridge or your country's famous bridges) use triangular trusses. How do engineers ensure these calculations are accurate?</p></li>
                    <li><p>How would your bridge design need to change if the span increased to <m>100\text{ cm}</m> instead of <m>60\text{ cm}</m>?</p></li>
                </ol>
                <solution component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        Expected responses:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p><strong>(i)</strong> Students should show the cosine rule or sine rule applied to calculate unknown side lengths or angles in their triangular trusses.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(ii)</strong> Triangles are rigid shapes; changing angles changes the triangle fundamentally, making them ideal for stable structures. Rectangles can deform into parallelograms without changing side lengths.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(iii)</strong> Decrease apex angles (make triangles flatter) to create wider load distribution; add diagonal bracing; use stronger materials; increase overall side lengths.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(iv)</strong> These bridges use complex truss networks; engineers use computer simulations, precise mathematical models, and stress testing to verify designs before construction.</p></li>
                        <li><p><strong>(v)</strong> Longer spans require more trusses or larger triangles; angles may need adjustment to maintain strength and proportional weight distribution.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </solution>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    <alert>Presentation and Engineering Report.</alert> Present your bridge design to the class as if you were engineers pitching to a client. Include:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Design sketches and scale drawings</p></li>
                        <li><p>Detailed trigonometric calculations justifying your design choices</p></li>
                        <li><p>Physical model or photographs of the constructed bridge</p></li>
                        <li><p>Load testing results and analysis</p></li>
                        <li><p>Answers to the reflection questions</p></li>
                        <li><p>Professional communication using correct mathematical terminology</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <note component="lesson-plan">
                    <p>
                        This develops not just mathematical skills but also communication, teamwork, and professional competencies valued in engineering and construction careers. Peer feedback can focus on both the mathematical accuracy and the clarity of explanations.
                    </p>
                </note>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
</activity>
    

